        "problem": "$\\int (\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x-(-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
